WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.640
From beloved childhood favorites to the sex
icon of a generation, these actors may be

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.327
gone but won't soon be forgotten!

00:00:08.327 --> 00:00:13.469
While technically the first live action portrayals
of the eponymous Mario brothers came in the

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:18.090
form of early '80s Atari commercials, "Captain"
Lou Albano's and Danny Wells' performance

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.810
as the daring duo is probably the most beloved.
They entertained millions of children beginning

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:27.650
in 1989 on "The Super Mario Bros. Super Show."
The show featured Albano and Wells playing

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.779
the two plumbers during live-action sequences
at their Brooklyn residence, often with a

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:36.210
celebrity guest, before switching over to
a cartoon episode of "Super Mario Bros." or

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:36.993
"The Legend of Zelda."

00:00:36.993 --> 00:00:42.149
Albano and Wells also voiced Mario and Luigi
in the "Mario Bros." cartoon episodes. Captain

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:46.720
Lou was known for a prominent professional
wrestling career that started in 1953 and

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.719
ended in 1995, and he was inducted into the
WWE Hall of Fame a year later. Albano died

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:56.829
on October 14, 2009, from a heart attack at
the age of 76.

00:00:56.829 --> 00:01:01.570
Danny Wells, who played Luigi, was known for
his TV guest appearances on the likes of "The

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:06.670
Jeffersons," "Happy Days," "Punky Brewster,"
"Chips," "The A-Team," "Sanford and Son,"

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:12.630
and "Starsky &amp; Hutch." He also did voice acting
for "Heathcliff," "Batman: The Animated Series,"

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:18.957
"Bakugan," "Turbo Dogs," and more. He died
of undisclosed causes on November 28, 2013.

00:01:18.957 --> 00:01:23.260
If you're one of the few people who made it
all the way through the "Jaws" franchise to

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:29.939
the fourth movie—1987's "Jaws: The Revenge"—you
may recognize Judith Barsi as the young granddaughter

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:31.532
of Police Chief Martin Brody.

00:01:31.532 --> 00:01:36.530
Before that movie came out, Barsi had already
had small parts in well-known TV series including

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:41.090
"Cheers" and "Cagney &amp; Lacey." Today, she
is best remembered for two Don Bluth feature

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:46.860
animations: She voiced Ducky, an excitable
young dinosaur, in 1988's "The Land Before

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:50.716
Time," and animal whisperer Anne-Marie in
"All Dogs Go to Heaven."

00:01:50.716 --> 00:01:57.329
On July 25, 1988, four months before "The
Land Before Time" premiered, Judith, age 10,

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:02.579
and her mother Maria were shot dead by Judith's
abusive father, who then killed himself. According

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:06.909
to the Los Angeles Times, a neighbor said
that Maria had lived in fear for herself and

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:11.237
her daughter. She was trying to leave her
husband but feared he would find and kill them.

00:02:11.237 --> 00:02:15.390
Both "The Land Before Time" and "All Dogs
Go to Heaven" came out after Judith and Maria's

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:20.018
deaths. Actor Heather Hogan voiced Ducky in
subsequent "Land Before Time" sequels.

00:02:20.018 --> 00:02:25.300
Dana Hill originally wanted to be a track
star until type I diabetes ended her youthful

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.220
athletic dreams. Her father's career as a
commercial director helped her start in the

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.990
industry, and she booked a series of ads before
appearing on various television programs,

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:38.720
including "Mork &amp; Mindy," "Fallen Angel,"
and "Magnum, P.I." She also voiced a variety

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.386
of animated characters for cartoons like "Rugrats,"
"Goof Troop," and "Darkwing Duck."

00:02:43.386 --> 00:02:48.520
She's best recognized for playing Audrey Griswold
in "National Lampoon's European Vacation,"

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:53.470
replacing Dana Barron from "National Lampoon's
Vacation" a few years prior. Hill's life and

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:58.310
career were both cut short after suffering
a fatal stroke triggered by her diabetes on

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.202
July 15, 1996, at the age of 32.

00:03:02.202 --> 00:03:07.409
A classically trained Shakespearian actor,
Helen McCrory made a name for herself on stage

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:10.827
and in a number of fan-favorite films and
television series.

00:03:10.827 --> 00:03:15.420
For those who don't frequent the London theater,
McCrory was probably best known for playing

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:20.280
Narcissa Malfoy in the "Harry Potter" film
franchise. Other film credits include "Skyfall,"

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:26.080
"Hugo," "The Queen," and more. On television,
McCrory played Madame Kali in "Penny Dreadful,"

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.640
provided the voice for Stelmaria on "His Dark
Materials," and owned every scene she appeared

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:36.250
in as Polly Gray on "Peaky Blinders." She
passed away on April 16, 2021, at the age

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:41.409
of 52 from breast cancer while filming the
sixth season of "Peaky Blinders," and certain

00:03:41.409 --> 00:03:45.444
storylines actually had to be rewritten due
to her untimely death.

00:03:45.444 --> 00:03:50.890
Funnyman Louie Anderson had one of the most
iconic voices in the world of comedy. He was

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:54.610
an actor whom you could identify after just
hearing a few words from his mouth.

00:03:54.610 --> 00:04:03.026
"'Well I just know it's a sound,' and he went,
'EHHHHH!' And the producer said, 'You mean…Louie Anderson?'"

00:04:03.026 --> 00:04:04.026
"Yeah!"

00:04:04.026 --> 00:04:09.060
Anderson's wit and quick-thinking delivery
made him a sought-after stand-up act—he

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:13.364
regularly appeared on late-night television
and celebrity game shows to help get the crowd rolling.

00:04:13.364 --> 00:04:18.750
Anderson's television career was an interesting
one. Early on, he created a cartoon series

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:23.750
called "Life With Louie," based on his childhood
in the Midwest. Anderson voiced both the childhood

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.889
version of himself and his father in the series.
The show won a number of Daytime Emmy awards

00:04:28.889 --> 00:04:34.470
in the mid-'90s. Anderson went on to appear
on many other shows in small, memorable roles

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:39.630
before becoming a critical darling again with
the FX series "Baskets." Anderson created

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:44.880
the show along with Zach Galifianakis, Louis
C.K., and Jonathan Krisel. He starred in the

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.524
series as Christine Baskets.

00:04:46.524 --> 00:04:53.200
Anderson died on January 21, 2022, from lymphoma
at the age of 68. He had kept his diagnosis

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:58.060
private for over a decade, and passed away
just three days after releasing information

00:04:58.060 --> 00:04:59.361
about his illness.

00:04:59.361 --> 00:05:04.580
Robbie Coltrane was a total chameleon of an
actor. His characters were capable of displaying

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:10.830
warmth, silliness, and love, while also showcasing
fearsome strength and darkness. To be able

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:16.010
to do this once or twice is impressive, but
to do it again and again requires some serious

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:20.030
talent. It's no wonder Coltrane was honored
as an Officer of the Order of the British

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:22.122
Empire in 2006.

00:05:22.122 --> 00:05:26.780
Coltrane jumped between comic and dramatic
roles with ease. His best-known characters

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:32.250
came from the world of film. He portrayed
Valentin Zukovsky, a former KGB officer and

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:37.190
frenemy of James Bond, in "Goldeneye" and
"The World Is Not Enough." His fame grew even

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:42.209
more when he was cast as gamekeeper Rubeus
Hagrid in the "Harry Potter" film franchise.

00:05:42.209 --> 00:05:46.280
Playing one of the warmest characters in a
beloved franchise helped cement Coltrane's

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:47.020
acting legacy.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.310
On the television side of things, Coltrane
regularly appeared in TV movies and miniseries

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:57.580
where he got to flex his acting muscles. He's
probably best known for the crime drama "Cracker,"

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.000
where he starred as Dr. Edward "Fitz" Fitzgerald.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:07.407
Coltrane died on October 14, 2022, due to
ongoing health complications. He was 72 years old.

00:06:07.407 --> 00:06:12.250
In the world of classic television, not many
shows stand above their peers like "Happy

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:18.680
Days." It ran for over a decade of television,
featuring 11 seasons and over 250 episodes,

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:23.650
and also helped launch the careers of notable
actors like Ron Howard, Henry Winkler, and

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:28.069
Scott Baio. One other actor who appeared in
every season of "Happy Days," and starred

00:06:28.069 --> 00:06:30.243
in one of its spin-offs, was Erin Moran.

00:06:30.243 --> 00:06:34.740
Moran played Joanie Cunningham, the younger
sister of Richie and Chuck, who eventually

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:39.190
starts dating Chachi. As the young couple
started gaining popularity on the show, a

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:43.750
spin-off series called "Joanie Loves Chachi"
was created. This new series was about a young

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:47.460
couple trying to start a musical act, and
the episodes featured performances from Baio

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:52.200
and Moran. "Joanie Loves Chachi" was not as
well-received as "Happy Days," and was canceled

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:57.180
during its second season. The characters popped
back up on "Happy Days" and actually got married

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:58.179
in the final episode.

00:06:58.179 --> 00:07:03.639
Moran continued to find work after "Happy
Days," but never really returned to the spotlight.

00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:09.380
She struggled with cancer later in life and
died in 2017 at the age of 56.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:12.590
Even if you've never seen something she's
appeared in, you've probably seen pictures

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:18.449
of Raquel Welch. Welch was a major sex symbol
in the 1960s and beyond. She made her first

00:07:18.449 --> 00:07:22.560
splash with the film "One Million Years B.C.,"
shooting to fame after appearing on the film's

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:28.479
poster clad only in an animal-skin bikini.
She only had a handful of lines in the film,

00:07:28.479 --> 00:07:31.751
but that image was more than enough to make
her a household name.

00:07:31.751 --> 00:07:37.110
Outside of that, Welch is probably best known
for roles in films like the 1973 version of

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:41.870
"The Three Musketeers," "Kansas City Bomber,"
"Bedazzled," and "Right to Die." She was a

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:46.810
regular guest on celebrity special`s and game
shows, and also had recurring roles on a handful

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:51.773
of short-lived series, like 2008's "Welcome
to the Captain" and 2017's "Date My Dad."

00:07:51.773 --> 00:07:56.598
Raquel Welch died on February 15, 2023, at
the age of 82.

00:07:56.598 --> 00:08:01.870
Whether you call him the Big Guy or Uncle
Phil, James Avery earned household name status

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:05.810
through his portrayal of Philip Banks on "The
Fresh Prince of Bel-Air." Prior to making

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:10.490
his mark on that long-running hit NBC sitcom,
the burly actor had plenty of small roles

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:15.809
throughout his TV career, although he was
particularly well known for his voice acting work.

00:08:15.809 --> 00:08:20.207
"You may call me, "The Shredder."

00:08:20.207 --> 00:08:25.490
Avery was the voice of Shredder on "Teenage
Mutant Ninja Turtles" and War Machine on "Iron

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:29.940
Man." He also did voices for a list of projects
that included "The Prince of Egypt," "Extreme

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:34.719
Ghostbusters," "Fist of the North Star," and
"Hulk Hogan's Rock 'n' Wrestling." A fatherly

00:08:34.719 --> 00:08:39.330
presence despite his most famous character's
name, Avery saddened a generation of viewers

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:44.959
when he died from complications following
heart surgery on December 31, 2013, at the

00:08:44.959 --> 00:08:46.281
age of 68.

00:08:46.281 --> 00:08:52.250
Wendie Jo Sperber had small parts in two very
big movies. She was an uncredited dancer in

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:56.760
"Grease," one of the most famous musicals
of all time. And she played Marty McFly's

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:01.232
older sister Linda in "Back to the Future,"
reprising the role for "Back to the Future Part III."

00:09:01.232 --> 00:09:05.400
Even before "Back to the Future," Sperber
was well known to some thanks to her part

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:10.820
in the sitcom "Bosom Buddies." The show starred
Tom Hanks as Kip and Peter Scolari as Henry,

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:14.930
two friends who have to pretend to be women
in order to get an apartment in a cheap women's-only

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:19.320
hotel. Sperber played Amy, their co-worker
and initially the only resident who knows

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:20.283
the men's secret.

00:09:20.283 --> 00:09:25.060
In addition to regular movie roles, Sperber
also appeared in episodes of various hit TV

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:29.323
series including "Murphy Brown," "Will &amp; Grace,"
"Home Improvement," and "JAG."

00:09:29.323 --> 00:09:34.620
Sperber was diagnosed with breast cancer in
1997. Four years later, she founded weSPARK

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:39.450
Cancer Center, a nonprofit intended to support
other patients and their families. She died

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:43.770
on November 29, 2005, at the age of 47.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:48.529
When it came to the characters on "Saved by
the Bell," the odd man out was often Sam Powers,

00:09:48.529 --> 00:09:53.329
better known as Screech. The "Saved by the
Bell" bunch boasted a nice collection of stereotyped

00:09:53.329 --> 00:09:57.920
characters, and Screech was the dork. Though
Screech typically appeared as a side character,

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.370
often aiding the schemes of Zack and Slater,
actor Dustin Diamond was still able to showcase

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:06.400
his comedic talents. That helped him carve
out a solid identity for a character that

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:09.406
could have easily been a throwaway member
of the central cast.

00:10:09.406 --> 00:10:14.270
Like his character, however, Diamond was kind
of the odd man out of the main cast. After

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:18.720
the show ended, Diamond struggled to land
substantial roles, and often found himself

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:24.360
at the center of bizarre controversies. A
sex tape was leaked in 2006 — one that Diamond

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:29.031
later admitted he himself released, with a
body double replacing him in most of the scenes.

00:10:29.031 --> 00:10:33.940
Diamond would also release a behind-the-scenes
tell-all book called "Behind the Bell," in

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:38.170
which he aired out all the dirty laundry on
his "Saved by the Bell" costars. He would

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:41.280
later admit that a ghostwriter made up almost
everything in the book.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.450
Diamond passed away from lung cancer in 2021.
He was 44 years old.

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:51.709
Earl Hindman had a relatively low-profile
show business career, but he stayed consistently

00:10:51.709 --> 00:10:56.300
busy for decades, appearing in movies like
"The Taking of Pelham One Two Three." Throughout

00:10:56.300 --> 00:11:01.170
the '70s and '80s, he had his most consistent
visibility in the part of Bob Reid, a character

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:07.360
he played in more than 450 episodes of the
long-running ABC daytime drama "Ryan's Hope."

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:12.796
It was a steady gig whose reliability and
dramatic potential would be the envy of most working actors.

00:11:12.796 --> 00:11:17.910
It was another supporting role on a long-running
series, however, that ultimately earned Hindman

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:19.480
his greatest notoriety.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.910
"Howdy, neighbor!"

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:26.940
Most '90s kids will remember him as Wilson,
the mysterious-but-friendly neighbor of Tim

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:32.100
"The Toolman" Taylor on the hit sitcom "Home
Improvement." Hindman died on December 29,

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:37.280
2003, at the age of 61 due to lung cancer,
leaving a generation with fond memories of

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:42.740
that lovable neighbor behind the fence who
always had the best advice and most interesting hobbies.

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:45.201
"Hey, Wilson what are you doing?"

00:11:45.201 --> 00:11:49.242
"Well, hi-ho Taylor teen! I am sculpting a
shrine out of yack butter."

00:11:49.242 --> 00:11:54.420
One of "The Original Kings of Comedy" next
to Cedric the Entertainer, D. L. Hughley,

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:59.030
and Steve Harvey, Bernie Mac built his hugely
successful career through a stand-up routine

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:04.529
heavily influenced by Richard Pryor. That
in and of itself isn't unusual but Mac used

00:12:04.529 --> 00:12:08.280
that cornerstone to build a presence and style
that was uniquely his own.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:14.231
"I never watched what somebody else does.
It does not matter. Bernie Mac gotta do his thing."

00:12:14.231 --> 00:12:19.080
That hard work paid off in Hollywood with
a series of high-profile roles. Many might

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:24.540
recognize Mac as Ice Cube's neighbor, Pastor
Clever, in the movie "Friday." He'd go on

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:29.490
to star in "Charlie's Angels: Full Throttle,"
"Ocean's Eleven," "Transformers," and more,

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:33.980
and also headlined a sitcom, loosely based
on his own experiences, called 'The Bernie

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:39.170
Mac Show'. During the last years of his life,
Mac suffered from Sarcoidosis, which heavily

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:47.046
affected his lungs, and he succumbed to pneumonia
complications on August 9, 2008, at the age of 50.

00:12:47.046 --> 00:12:52.937
Harold Ramis was 69 when he passed away from
an autoimmune disease in 2014, which in and

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:58.360
of itself might have come as a surprise to
many of the film fans who watched him in '80s

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:02.701
classics like "Stripes," "National Lampoon's
Vacation," and "Ghostbusters." A talented

00:13:02.701 --> 00:13:07.220
director and screenwriter as well as a frequent
fixture in some of the biggest comedy hits

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:12.660
of the decade, Ramis' behind-the-scenes talents
helped bring us "Groundhog Day" and "Caddyshack,"

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:16.940
among many others; in fact, he jumped back
and forth between either side of the cameras

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:21.401
throughout his career, co-writing both of
the "Ghostbusters" movies as well as "Stripes."

00:13:21.401 --> 00:13:26.199
Although few fans knew he was ill, Ramis struggled
with the side effects of his infection for

00:13:26.199 --> 00:13:30.820
years—he had to learn how to walk all over
again after contracting autoimmune inflammatory

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:36.779
vasculitis in 2010, and suffered a relapse
the following year. His final onscreen appearance

00:13:36.779 --> 00:13:39.790
came in the 2009 comedy Year One.

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:45.230
A veteran of stage and screen who amassed
a distinguished career spanning decades, Richard

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:50.660
Harris notched arguably his highest-profile
role late in his career, courtesy of the blockbuster

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:55.740
"Harry Potter" film franchise. In two of his
final screen appearances, Harris played Potter's

00:13:55.740 --> 00:14:00.230
beloved headmaster Albus Dumbledore in the
first two installments of the blockbuster

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:03.875
film franchise, "The Sorcerer's Stone" and
"The Chamber of Secrets."

00:14:03.875 --> 00:14:09.580
Although he'll always be Dumbledore to generations
of Potter-loving fans, outside of Hogwarts,

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:14.880
Harris compiled multiple careers' worth of
impressive credits and honors. He was an accomplished

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:20.580
singer, actor, director, writer, producer,
and songwriter who won Grammy and Golden Globe

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:25.269
awards for his work on 15 different albums
and more than 70 films, including "Camelot,"

00:14:25.269 --> 00:14:30.040
"The Guns of Navarone," "Gulliver's Travels,"
"Patriot Games," "Unforgiven," "Gladiator,"

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:35.970
and "The Count of Monte Cristo." After being
diagnosed with Hodgkin's disease, Harris died

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:40.034
on October 25, 2002 at the age of 72.

00:14:40.034 --> 00:14:44.790
Best known for playing the young Carol Anne
from the movie "Poltergeist," Heather O'Rourke

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:49.680
skyrocketed to instant fame as the sweet young
girl that terrified audiences after being

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:54.960
sucked into the television set. In fact, she
may have played the part a little too well:

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:59.089
although O'Rourke racked up an impressive
list of credits after her breakthrough performance

00:14:59.089 --> 00:15:03.680
as Carol Anne, she remained tied to the franchise
in the public's mind, and she was one of a

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:07.940
very small number of original cast members
that remained with the movie series until

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:10.902
its conclusion with 1988's "Poltergeist III."

00:15:10.902 --> 00:15:15.480
If you've seen the final movie, you may notice
that Heather's cheeks appear very puffy—and

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:19.600
although audiences didn't know it at the time,
it was a reaction to the medication she was

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:25.519
taking for Crohn's disease, with which she
was misdiagnosed. Tragedy struck on February

00:15:25.519 --> 00:15:30.670
1, 1988, when Heather suffered from cardiac
arrest while undergoing a surgery for a bowel

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:33.240
obstruction at the tender age of 12.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:38.041
Thuy Trang was best known for her work as
the original Yellow Ranger on "Mighty Morphin

00:15:38.041 --> 00:15:42.850
Power Rangers," but unlike a lot of actors
who start out in genre TV, she seemed in no

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:47.740
danger of being typecast; in fact, she was
developing what seemed like a potentially

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:52.630
promising career after leaving the show. Trang
jumped right into feature work, landing roles

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:57.870
in a pair of movies released in 1996: the
dark drama "The Crow: City of Angels" and

00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:00.342
the Leslie Neilsen comedy "Spy Hard."

00:16:00.342 --> 00:16:04.940
Clearly, Trang had enough chops to tackle
work from a variety of genres, but sadly,

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:09.519
she never had a chance to fulfill that potential—she
was killed in a car accident at the age of

00:16:09.519 --> 00:16:15.070
27 in 2001, after the driver lost control
of the vehicle and it plunged over a guardrail

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:20.790
on California's Interstate 5. Emergency medical
personnel tried getting Trang to the hospital,

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:24.854
but she died in transit, the result of massive
internal injuries.

00:16:24.854 --> 00:16:30.720
One of Hollywood's all-time ultimate 'That
Guy' actors, Taylor Negron compiled a truly

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:35.221
impressive list of classic screen credits
without ever becoming a household name. Although

00:16:35.221 --> 00:16:39.550
he tended to be on screen for no more than
a few moments at a time, Negron's deadpan

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:44.639
delivery made him a fan favorite among film
aficionados of the '80s—and a standout in

00:16:44.639 --> 00:16:48.414
pictures like "Fast Times at Ridgemont High"
and "Better Off Dead".

00:16:48.414 --> 00:16:53.310
Later in his career, Negron made memorable
appearances on hit TV shows like "Seinfeld"

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:57.449
and "Friends", but as much fun as he could
be onscreen, he was even more interesting

00:16:57.449 --> 00:17:02.120
behind the scenes. In addition to acting,
Negron was an acclaimed writer whose work

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:06.370
included published collections of short stories,
as well as a trained painter whose work was

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:12.130
shown in multiple galleries. He was only 57
when he died in early 2015, after a battle

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:13.341
with liver cancer.

00:17:13.341 --> 00:17:18.600
After making a name for himself as a professional
wrestler, "Rowdy" Roddy Piper branched out

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:23.949
onto the big screen, most notably through
his starring role in the 1988 cult classic

00:17:23.949 --> 00:17:29.620
"They Live". Leading that cult John Carpenter
classic, Piper waged war against a covert

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:32.710
alien invasion and coined an unforgettable
catchphrase.

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:39.761
"I have come here to chew bubble gum and kick
ass, and I'm all out of bubble gum."

00:17:39.761 --> 00:17:45.370
Piper's bone-crunching work in that film helped
set the tone for a busy Hollywood career full

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:50.360
of B-movie roles and small-but-unforgettable
appearances on television shows like "It's

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:55.309
Always Sunny in Philadelphia." On the screen
as well as in the ring, Piper gave every performance

00:17:55.309 --> 00:18:00.520
his all, frequently elevating otherwise forgettable
material and consistently serving as the most

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:06.200
memorable component of whatever project he
was appearing in. He died in his sleep in

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:10.789
2015 after suffering a pulmonary embolism
that triggered a heart attack.

00:18:10.789 --> 00:18:16.330
Critically maligned and pretty much ignored
at the box office, "Rocky V" is indisputably

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:22.000
the least loved film of Sylvester Stallone's
long-running boxing franchise. Take a careful

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:26.780
look under its cheesier elements, however,
and you can see the outline of a truly affecting

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:31.650
story about a former heavyweight champ going
broke, losing his ability to box, and nearly

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:33.630
losing his relationship with his son.

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:38.300
To help tell the story, Stallone brought in
his own 14-year-old son, Sage, to play the

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:43.360
part of Robert "Rocky Jr." Balboa in the film,
and the young Stallone's time on the set clearly

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:47.970
made a positive impression: after college,
Sage continued working in the industry in

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:52.960
small, low-key projects, carving out a career
in his own right apart from his blockbuster-leading

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:58.809
father. Unfortunately, Sage died on July 13,
2012, at the young age of 36 due to a heart

00:18:58.809 --> 00:19:01.761
attack brought on by coronary artery disease.

00:19:01.761 --> 00:19:06.900
Long before he founded an ill-fated amusement
park using the secrets of dino DNA, Richard

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:12.360
Attenborough was already an accomplished and
award-winning actor. Attenborough starred

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:17.230
alongside Steve McQueen in "The Great Escape",
released in the midst of a busy period that

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:22.791
included some of his most widely seen work.
He also won two consecutive Golden Globe Awards

00:19:22.791 --> 00:19:28.350
in 1967 and 1968 for co-starring in "The Sand
Pebbles" and "Doctor Dolittle".

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:33.200
Needless to say, Attenborough's career contains
no shortage of highlights, and his work will

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:37.990
no doubt be familiar to classic film fans.
Younger filmgoers, however, have their own

00:19:37.990 --> 00:19:42.080
Attenborough memories thanks to his appearance
as John Hammond, whose ambitions led to the

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:46.460
creation of Jurassic Park. That blockbuster
made him famous to a whole new generation,

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:50.370
but it's just one short chapter in a life
of many great cinematic achievements that

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:54.419
ended on August 24, 2014 at the age of 90.

00:19:54.419 --> 00:19:56.544
"Whatchoo talkin' 'bout Willis?"

00:19:56.544 --> 00:20:01.860
Kids of the '70s and '80s and honestly anyone
who enjoys the classic sitcoms of the era

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:07.299
will immediately recognize the immortal catchphrase
Gary Coleman coined as an adorable kid on

00:20:07.299 --> 00:20:09.773
the long-running hit sitcom "Diff'rent Strokes."

00:20:09.773 --> 00:20:14.179
Coleman's diminutive stature helped him continue
to play younger characters throughout much

00:20:14.179 --> 00:20:18.710
of his career, and at the peak of his fame,
he landed a series of film and television

00:20:18.710 --> 00:20:24.780
roles. Unfortunately, his youthful looks and
peak height of 4 feet 8 inches were side effects

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:28.870
of an autoimmune kidney disease, combined
with the medications he took throughout his

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:34.390
life to fight the condition. Work in Hollywood
became harder to come by after "Strokes" left

00:20:34.390 --> 00:20:39.130
the airwaves, and behind the scenes, he underwent
frequent dialysis treatments, and was forced

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:44.990
to undergo two kidney transplants. After a
fall at his home, Coleman died on May 28,

00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:49.220
2010 of an epidural hematoma at the age of
42.

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:54.020
Exactly where you remember esteemed actor
Edward Herrmann from depends on whether you

00:20:54.020 --> 00:21:00.000
favor musicals, vampire movies, medical TV
shows, or fast-talking mother-daughter family dramas.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:05.669
Herrmann is probably best recognized today
for his role as pompous but warm-hearted patriarch

00:21:05.669 --> 00:21:10.910
Richard Gilmore in the series "Gilmore Girls."
Richard's dry sense of humor and quiet authority

00:21:10.910 --> 00:21:15.980
contrasted with the quick wit and constant
banter that flew between his daughter Lorelai,

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:20.046
his granddaughter Rory and his wife Emily,
who was played by Kelly Bishop.

00:21:20.046 --> 00:21:24.700
Offscreen, Herrmann and Bishop, both classically
trained and meticulous about their craft,

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:29.919
were especially close. Herrmann's wife Star,
whom he married in 1994, would joke that Bishop

00:21:29.919 --> 00:21:35.562
was his other wife. In Herrmann's final moments,
his family invited Bishop to say goodbye with them.

00:21:35.562 --> 00:21:40.510
Before his longest-running role in "Gilmore
Girls," Herrmann worked regularly on TV and

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:45.690
in movies. He was nominated for an Emmy for
playing a priest in the medical drama "St.

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:50.789
Elsewhere," and won one for the legal series
"The Practice." He played Franklin D. Roosevelt

00:21:50.789 --> 00:21:56.280
in the 1982 adaptation of "Annie" — having
previously played the President for a miniseries

00:21:56.280 --> 00:22:01.930
and TV movie in the late '70s. And in 1987,
he played a stepfather from literal Hell in

00:22:01.930 --> 00:22:03.921
the vampire movie "The Lost Boys."

00:22:03.921 --> 00:22:11.200
Herrmann died of brain cancer, at age 71 on
December 21, 2014. In the Netflix revival

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:17.431
of "Gilmore Girls," it was revealed that Richard
had also died, sparking big changes for Emily in particular.

00:22:17.431 --> 00:22:24.090
Deezer D was one of the foundational actors
on hit medical drama "ER." Born Dearon Thompson,

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:30.708
he played nurse Malik McGrath for the show's
entire 15-year run, appearing in 188 episodes.

00:22:30.708 --> 00:22:35.250
As often happens in real hospitals as well
as fictional ones, "ER" typically focused

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:40.510
on its physicians and surgeons, not the nurses.
But Thompson was one of just a handful of

00:22:40.510 --> 00:22:45.360
actors who appeared in every season, and his
episode count makes him the fifth most prolific

00:22:45.360 --> 00:22:47.158
member of the large cast.

00:22:47.158 --> 00:22:51.590
Outside of "ER," Thompson had a few small
parts in movies including the cult classic

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:56.090
comedy "Romy and Michele's High School Reunion"
and appeared in Vanilla Ice's video for "Cool

00:22:56.090 --> 00:23:01.029
as Ice." He played one of the members of a
fictional rap group in the mockumentary movie

00:23:01.029 --> 00:23:06.840
"CB4" in 1993, and also rapped for real. He
released multiple records, including the full-length

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:12.330
album "Delayed, But Not Denied" in 2008, and
continued making songs until his death. His

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:15.929
final track, "History Can't Be Stopped," was
released posthumously.

00:23:15.929 --> 00:23:20.549
Thompson started having heart issues around
2008, telling RadarOnline the following year

00:23:20.549 --> 00:23:25.140
that he had been suffering from recurrent
pneumonia and heart failure. It was discovered

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:30.635
that he had a leaking valve and expanded aorta,
which was addressed with surgery in 2009.

00:23:30.635 --> 00:23:34.960
According to Deadline, he went on to have
more operations on his heart over the next

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:42.199
10 years or so. Thompson died on January 7,
2021, at the age of 55, of a suspected heart attack.

00:23:42.199 --> 00:23:48.240
Kathryn Joosten was in her 50s when she started
working consistently in notoriously youth-centric

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:53.630
Hollywood in the mid 1990s. She'd started
acting in 1980, after realizing she was missing

00:23:53.630 --> 00:23:55.454
out on following her dreams.

00:23:55.454 --> 00:24:00.710
Despite her late start, Joosten built a career
playing one-off but always memorable characters

00:24:00.710 --> 00:24:05.920
in hit TV series. Her credits include "ER,"
"3rd Rock From the Sun," "Seinfeld,'' "Roseanne,"

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:10.039
"The Nanny," "Frasier," "Buffy the Vampire
Slayer," and "The X Files," to name just a

00:24:10.039 --> 00:24:14.213
few. Later she would add the likes of "Will
&amp; Grace" and "Grey's Anatomy" to that list.

00:24:14.213 --> 00:24:19.480
In short, name a hit show of the '90s and
'00s, and Joosten probably appeared in it.

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:23.860
Her most memorable guest appearance was as
a woman who refused treatment for kidney failure

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:29.100
in Season 1 of "Scrubs." The episode, "My
Old Lady," consistently ranks as a fan favorite,

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.679
thanks in no small part to Joosten's performance.

00:24:31.679 --> 00:24:33.820
"Unfortunately we don't really have a choice."

00:24:33.820 --> 00:24:36.000
"Actually I do have a choice."

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:39.580
"Certain things you never expect to come out
of a person's mouth."

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:42.560
"I think I'm ready to die."

00:24:42.560 --> 00:24:46.920
In addition to those guest roles, Joosten
landed regular parts in two major series.

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:50.950
She played the steely secretary to Martin
Sheen's President Bartlet in two seasons of

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:55.950
"The West Wing," combining her talents for
fast-paced drama and comedy, and nosy but

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:59.713
occasionally helpful neighbor Karen McCluskey
in "Desperate Housewives."

00:24:59.713 --> 00:25:05.250
Joosten was first treated for lung cancer
in 2001, the year "My Old Lady" aired. She

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:08.960
was undergoing treatment while making the
final season of "Desperate Housewives," which

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:14.419
was written into her character's arc. McCluskey
died in the series finale. Joosten died two

00:25:14.419 --> 00:25:19.628
weeks after that episode aired, on June 2,
2012, at the age of 72.

00:25:19.628 --> 00:25:25.720
Hugh Dane's resume reads like a list of the
top sitcoms of the '90s and early '00s. He

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:29.200
played two different characters in "The Fresh
Prince of Bel-Air," appeared in an episode

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:34.039
of "Martin," and multiple episodes of "Roc."
He had small parts in "Friends," "Sister,

00:25:34.039 --> 00:25:38.980
Sister," "Everybody Hates Chris," and "New
Girl." On the drama side, he played a judge

00:25:38.980 --> 00:25:43.690
in an episode of "The West Wing." He also
appeared briefly in the comedy movies "Little

00:25:43.690 --> 00:25:45.220
Fockers" and "Bridesmaids."

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:49.950
However, Dane will most likely be remembered
and missed for his role as Hank, the permanently

00:25:49.950 --> 00:25:55.790
unimpressed security guard in "The Office."
He appeared in 22 episodes of NBC's hit mockumentary

00:25:55.790 --> 00:26:01.184
about the inner workings and ridiculous shenanigans
in a Scranton, Pennsylvania paper company.

00:26:01.184 --> 00:26:09.130
Dane died at the age of 75 on May 16, 2018.
After his death, his "The Office" co-star

00:26:09.130 --> 00:26:14.860
Rainn Wilson described him as "one of the
greats. So kind, funny, talented." On January

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:20.860
1, 2021, NBC's streaming platform Peacock
tweeted a video of a cold open that never

00:26:20.860 --> 00:26:26.140
aired, dedicated to Dane. It showed one of
Jim's most elaborate pranks on nemesis Dwight,

00:26:26.140 --> 00:26:28.520
featuring Dane as Morpheus from "The Matrix."

00:26:28.520 --> 00:26:32.619
"If the blue pill's not safe to swallow, someone
should tell us now!"

00:26:32.619 --> 00:26:37.530
Kenny Baker originated one of the most beloved
characters in one of the most popular movie

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:43.200
franchises of all time — but you wouldn't
recognize him from that role out of costume.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:47.690
Baker is most famous for playing R2-D2, the
brave and mischievous "Star Wars" droid. He

00:26:47.690 --> 00:26:52.799
was a dwarf, but it wasn't just Baker's height
that made him perfect for the role. According

00:26:52.799 --> 00:26:57.970
to his New York Times obituary, he was the
only person small enough to fit into the costume

00:26:57.970 --> 00:27:00.320
who was also strong enough to move it.

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:05.799
It helped that Baker had been in entertainment
for years: He sang, danced, played instruments,

00:27:05.799 --> 00:27:10.350
clowned, and ice skated in various touring
companies. As he told the Guardian -

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:15.309
"There are certain closeup movements only
I can do convincingly. It's all about giving

00:27:15.309 --> 00:27:19.014
R2 a personality, and CGI can never really
do that."

00:27:19.014 --> 00:27:23.900
In addition to R2, Baker played an Ewok in
"Return of the Jedi," alongside his wife and

00:27:23.900 --> 00:27:28.700
fellow actor Eileen. But the furry, violent
inhabitants of the jungle planet Endor were

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:33.210
less fun to play than to watch, thanks to
the stuffy costumes. It wasn't just that they

00:27:33.210 --> 00:27:37.059
were hot: The heads were impossible to see
out of when they steamed up.

00:27:37.059 --> 00:27:42.710
The last time Baker played R2 was for "Revenge
of the Sith," although he served as a consultant

00:27:42.710 --> 00:27:47.649
on "The Force Awakens." He also appeared in
many other movies and series, including "Flash

00:27:47.649 --> 00:27:54.439
Gordon," "Time Bandits" and "Amadeus." He
died at the age of 81 on August 13, 2016.

00:27:54.439 --> 00:27:59.039
If you're a millennial who grew up wishing
you could have an identical twin to swap lives

00:27:59.039 --> 00:28:04.120
with, you probably remember Natasha Richardson
best for her role as the extremely cool fashion

00:28:04.120 --> 00:28:07.648
designer mom in the Lindsay Lohan-led remake
of "The Parent Trap."

00:28:07.648 --> 00:28:12.567
That was Richardson's most prominent screen
role, but she had a prestigious stage career.

00:28:12.567 --> 00:28:17.380
It started with a touring version of "The
Seagull" which ended in London's West End,

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:22.120
with Richardson appearing opposite her mother,
renowned British actor Vanessa Redgrave.

00:28:22.120 --> 00:28:27.000
TV fans may know Vanessa best for her role
as Dr. Erica Noughton in "Nip/Tuck," opposite

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:32.480
her other daughter and Natasha's sister, Joely
Richardson. Joely should also be known to

00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:39.042
fans from "The Parent Trap" era as Anita in
the 1996 live-action adaptation of "101 Dalmatians."

00:28:39.042 --> 00:28:43.820
As you've probably noted, Natasha was part
of an acting dynasty. Her grandfather Sir

00:28:43.820 --> 00:28:49.299
Michael Redgrave, Vanessa's father, was famous
for tragedies. Natasha's aunt, Lynn Redgrave,

00:28:49.299 --> 00:28:54.149
Vanessa's sister, was an Oscar-nominated theater
superstar. To round out the family, Lynn and

00:28:54.149 --> 00:28:57.679
Vanessa's brother, Corin Redgrave, was a stage
actor and playwright.

00:28:57.679 --> 00:29:02.140
Natasha met actor Liam Neeson when they starred
opposite each other in a Broadway production

00:29:02.140 --> 00:29:06.760
of "Anna Christie." She was nominated for
a Tony for the role, and the two later married

00:29:06.760 --> 00:29:08.620
and had two sons together.

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:16.417
Natasha died suddenly of head injuries sustained
during a skiing trip on March 18, 2009. She was 45.

00:29:16.417 --> 00:29:21.210
In a show that made superstars out of its
six main cast members, James Michael Tyler

00:29:21.210 --> 00:29:25.590
managed to hold his own as one of the most
memorable side characters.

00:29:25.590 --> 00:29:31.140
He played coffee shop manager Gunther in "Friends"
for all 10 seasons, appearing in 150 episodes

00:29:31.140 --> 00:29:36.190
total, although he didn't have a line for
his first 33 appearances. Tyler originally

00:29:36.190 --> 00:29:40.464
landed the role because he knew how to work
the fancy espresso machine on the Central Perk set.

00:29:40.464 --> 00:29:44.750
Not one to take himself overly seriously,
after "Friends," he played himself in the

00:29:44.750 --> 00:29:49.470
satire "Episodes," which saw that version
of Tyler experiencing a slightly awkward reunion

00:29:49.470 --> 00:29:53.315
with Matt LeBlanc, who played Joey on "Friends,"
also playing himself.

00:29:53.315 --> 00:29:58.410
Tyler loved the experience of being in "Friends,"
although there was one drawback. As he told

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:04.289
Digital Spy, he was cast with very distinctive
bleached blonde hair, which added to his character's

00:30:04.289 --> 00:30:08.480
mystique. But it was originally the result
of an experiment he let one of his friends

00:30:08.480 --> 00:30:11.681
perform, which turned into weekly bleach jobs
for 10 years.

00:30:11.681 --> 00:30:17.549
Tyler was diagnosed with prostate cancer in
2018, which he didn't make public until June

00:30:17.549 --> 00:30:23.490
2021. That month, he announced in an interview
with NBC that the cancer had reached stage

00:30:23.490 --> 00:30:28.419
four, meaning it had spread to other parts
of his body. He died just a few months later,

00:30:28.419 --> 00:30:31.029
on October 24, 2021.

00:30:31.029 --> 00:30:36.659
A veteran stage, film, and television actor,
Richard Griffiths is known by the masses for

00:30:36.659 --> 00:30:42.049
his portrayal of Harry Potter's uncle, Vernon
Dursley. While Dursley was for the most part

00:30:42.049 --> 00:30:46.210
always cruel to the young wizard, he was still
a part of Harry's family—and he ultimately

00:30:46.210 --> 00:30:50.799
helped prove that the Boy Who Lived had a
golden heart because he still cared for the

00:30:50.799 --> 00:30:52.349
uncle who treated him poorly.

00:30:52.349 --> 00:30:57.341
Outside of the "Harry Potter" series, Griffiths
won a slew of awards over the course of a

00:30:57.341 --> 00:31:01.799
busy career, earning particular acclaim for
his portrayal of English teacher Douglas Hector

00:31:01.799 --> 00:31:07.190
in a play called "The History Boys." He also
starred in a variety of projects, including

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:11.799
"Superman II," "Gandhi," "Sleepy Hollow,"
"King Ralph," "Hugo," "Pirates of the Caribbean:

00:31:11.799 --> 00:31:16.220
On Stranger Tides," and "The Hitchhiker's
Guide to the Galaxy." Griffiths passed away

00:31:16.220 --> 00:31:23.740
at 65 on March 28, 2013 due to complications
shortly after having heart surgery.

00:31:23.740 --> 00:31:28.559
Brad Renfro skyrocketed to fame at age 11
through his acclaimed work in Joel Schumacher's

00:31:28.559 --> 00:31:34.050
adaptation of author John Grisham's book "The
Client." High profile roles quickly followed,

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:38.740
and Renfro made appearances in "Sleepers"
opposite Brad Pitt, "The Informers" starring

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:42.370
Kim Basinger, and "Ghost World" alongside
Scarlett Johansson.

00:31:42.370 --> 00:31:48.019
Yet as his star continued to rise on screen,
Renfro faced a darker path behind the scenes,

00:31:48.019 --> 00:31:52.649
one that would ultimately see him fulfilling
the sad destiny of many child actors, plunging

00:31:52.649 --> 00:31:57.320
into a life of illegal drug use and trouble
with the law. After attempting to buy drugs

00:31:57.320 --> 00:32:01.830
from an undercover L.A. police officer, Renfro
pleaded no contest to attempted possession

00:32:01.830 --> 00:32:08.289
of heroin and spent ten days in jail. On January
15, 2008, at 25 years old, Renfro's turbulent

00:32:08.289 --> 00:32:12.840
story reached a tragic end when he was found
dead of an accidental heroin and morphine

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:15.389
overdose in his apartment in Los Angeles.

00:32:15.389 --> 00:32:20.880
Dana Plato played Kimberly Drummond in the
long-running hit television series Diff'rent

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:26.100
Strokes. Cast alongside fellow child stars
Gary Coleman and Todd Bridges, she was catapulted

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:31.720
to instant stardom. To cope with the mounting
pressures of fame, Plato often indulged in

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:36.160
cocaine and marijuana off the set—yet it
wasn't until she became pregnant that the

00:32:36.160 --> 00:32:41.789
network released her from her contract, helping
trigger a downward spiral of drugs and alcohol

00:32:41.789 --> 00:32:45.549
that included work in adult films and a spread
in Playboy magazine.

00:32:45.549 --> 00:32:50.309
She later moved to Las Vegas, where, desperate
for cash, she robbed a store for less than

00:32:50.309 --> 00:32:56.970
$200 and was bonded out of jail by Vegas entertainer
Wayne Newton. She died at the age of 35 on

00:32:56.970 --> 00:33:04.380
May 8, 1999, in her RV in Oklahoma after taking
a fatal dose of lortab and valium.

00:33:04.380 --> 00:33:08.600
Best known for her work starring as Mel Gibson's
daughter in the blockbuster "The Patriot,"

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:13.340
Skye McCole Bartusiak was a child actress
with a promising future. She made her film

00:33:13.340 --> 00:33:17.760
debut in "The Cider House Rules" in 1999 and
starred with Michael Douglas and Brittany

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:22.909
Murphy in "Don't Say a Word" in 2001. She
also appeared in the film "Kill your Darlings"

00:33:22.909 --> 00:33:26.649
with "True Blood" star Alexander Skarsgard
in 2006.

00:33:26.649 --> 00:33:32.170
Unfortunately, Bartusiak's life behind the
scenes wasn't as glamorous as fans might have

00:33:32.170 --> 00:33:36.130
assumed—and included some dangerous drug
habits that would end up contributing to her

00:33:36.130 --> 00:33:41.580
having an accidental overdose in 2014. According
to Bartusiak's mother, the young star had

00:33:41.580 --> 00:33:46.679
also been suffering from epileptic seizures
prior to her passing, which led the family

00:33:46.679 --> 00:33:50.519
to initially believe she'd choked to death
before the coroner's ruling made the tragic

00:33:50.519 --> 00:33:51.620
truth clear.

00:33:51.620 --> 00:33:57.313
Before getting his big break, Michael K. Williams
was consistently cast as one-dimensional criminals.

00:33:57.313 --> 00:34:01.690
He was a young Black man in a white industry
that didn't know how to tell diverse stories

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:06.260
about people who looked like him. Also, he
had a distinctive facial scar from the time

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:11.169
someone attacked him with a razor–something
else Hollywood didn't know what to do with.

00:34:11.169 --> 00:34:15.250
Williams' big break came courtesy of "The
Wire" character Omar Little, an armed robber

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:20.501
who targets drug dealers, and the most feared
man in a territory ruled by violence. But

00:34:20.501 --> 00:34:25.609
Little wasn't a carbon copy of those previous
characters. For one thing, he was openly gay,

00:34:25.609 --> 00:34:30.099
in a homophobic society that mocked gay men
as weak. For another, he was known to give

00:34:30.099 --> 00:34:34.669
some of his ill-gotten gains to people in
his much-in-need community.

00:34:34.669 --> 00:34:38.889
After playing Omar, Williams landed roles
in several prestigious series, including crime

00:34:38.889 --> 00:34:43.720
drama "The Night Of," "When We Rise," about
the gay civil rights movement, and a recurring

00:34:43.720 --> 00:34:49.169
part as a racketeer in Prohibition drama "Boardwalk
Empire." He even got to show off his comedy

00:34:49.169 --> 00:34:54.770
skills in "Community." In 2019, he appeared
in Ava DuVernay's "When They See Us," as the

00:34:54.770 --> 00:35:01.010
father of one of the wrongly accused Central
Park Five. In 2021, he was nominated for his

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:04.859
fifth Emmy for the HBO series "Lovecraft County."

00:35:04.859 --> 00:35:11.211
Williams died at the age of 54 on September
6, 2021, of what was later confirmed as a drug overdose.

00:35:11.211 --> 00:35:16.010
The Famous Jett Jackson, Lee Thompson Young's
star vehicle, was a very popular show on The

00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:20.332
Disney Channel for some time. Friday Night
Lights, Akeelah and the Bee, and The Hills

00:35:20.332 --> 00:35:24.420
Have Eyes II are some of the few films on
Young's impressive resume. But while working

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:29.540
as a series regular on Rizzoli and Isles,
he was diagnosed with Bipolar Disorder. Sadly,

00:35:29.540 --> 00:35:35.440
on August 19, 2013, his life came to a tragic
end after he committed suicide with a self-inflicted

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:36.440
gunshot wound.

00:35:36.612 --> 00:35:41.284
Dominique Dunne was born into a life of privilege
in Santa Monica to a ranching heiress and

00:35:41.284 --> 00:35:46.587
TV writer and producer Dominick Dunne. Dominique
made appearances in the TV shows Chips, Fame,

00:35:46.587 --> 00:35:51.710
and The Quest. But the same year Poltergeist
was released, her deranged ex-boyfriend, John

00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:56.610
Thomas Sweeney, murdered her in her own driveway
in West Hollywood on November 4, 1982.

00:35:56.610 --> 00:36:01.410
With a resume as long as your arm, Jonathan
Brandis showed up in Who's the Boss?, Blossom,

00:36:01.410 --> 00:36:06.200
L.A. Law, Full House, The Wonder Years, and
Murder, She Wrote, among tons of others. As

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:10.640
a child and teenage star for many years, it
seemed like he had no shortage of work. He

00:36:10.640 --> 00:36:15.790
was 16 when he made Ladybugs, and 17 when
he was cast as a teen genius in Steven Spielberg's

00:36:15.790 --> 00:36:21.930
SeaQuest 2032. When SeaQuest was canceled
in 1996, however, the once-plentiful casting

00:36:21.930 --> 00:36:26.990
offers dried up. Depression soon plagued the
young star. He died after sustaining injuries

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:32.902
from an attempted suicide via hanging at the
age of 27 in Danbury, Connecticut.

00:36:32.902 --> 00:36:37.220
Kelly made audiences laugh as part of the
ensemble cast of That '70s Show, but her real

00:36:37.220 --> 00:36:41.460
life wasn't funny at all. After a lengthy
battle with drug and alcohol addiction that

00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:46.690
ultimately caused her to lose her job on the
show, Kelly checked into rehab in August 2013,

00:36:46.690 --> 00:36:52.236
only to pass away in her sleep due to complications
from an accidental overdose. She was only 43.

00:36:52.236 --> 00:36:57.790
Duncan's towering physique made him a natural
for "big guy" roles, but he really had dramatic

00:36:57.790 --> 00:37:01.599
chops—and unlike a lot of actors booked
for that type of part, he got a chance to

00:37:01.599 --> 00:37:06.359
show them. His too-brief filmography includes
memorable supporting turns in Armageddon and

00:37:06.359 --> 00:37:10.210
Daredevil, as well as a beautifully sensitive
starring performance in the big-screen adaptation

00:37:10.210 --> 00:37:14.790
of Stephen King's The Green Mile. Duncan should
have had a much longer career, but he was

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:17.849
felled by a heart attack in 2012 at the age
of 54.

